'''Westley Watende Omari Moore''' (born October 15, 1978) is an American politician, businessman, author, and [[U.S. Army]] veteran serving as the 63rd [[governor of Maryland]] since 2023.

Moore was born in Maryland and raised primarily in New York. He graduated from [[Johns Hopkins University]] and received a [[master's degree]] from [[Wolfson College, Oxford]], as a [[Rhodes Scholar]]. After several years in the [[United States Army|U.S. Army]] and [[United States Army Reserve|Army Reserve]], he became an investment banker in New York. Between 2010 and 2015, Moore published five books, including a young-adult novel. He served as CEO of the [[Robin Hood Foundation]] from 2017 to 2021.<ref name="leaving3">{{Cite web |last=McLeod |first=Ethan |date=February 8, 2021 |title=Wes Moore stepping down as CEO of New York's Robin Hood Foundation |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/baltimore/news/2021/02/08/wes-moore-leaving-ceo-post-robin-hood-foundation.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210208224451/https://www.bizjournals.com/baltimore/news/2021/02/08/wes-moore-leaving-ceo-post-robin-hood-foundation.html |archive-date=February 8, 2021 |access-date=May 13, 2021 |website=[[Baltimore Business Journal]]}}</ref> Moore authored ''[[The Other Wes Moore]]'' and ''The Work.'' He also hosted ''Beyond Belief'' on the [[Oprah Winfrey Network (U.S. TV channel)|Oprah Winfrey Network]] (OWN), and was executive producer and a writer for ''Coming Back with Wes Moore'' on [[PBS]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Moore |first1=Wes |title=Coming Back With Wes Moore |url=https://www.pbs.org/coming-back-with-wes-moore/home/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150815153013/https://www.pbs.org/coming-back-with-wes-moore/home/ |archive-date=August 15, 2015 |access-date=August 18, 2015 |website=PBS.org}}</ref>

Moore is a member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]]. He won the [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election]], becoming Maryland's first black governor and the third black person elected governor of any U.S. state.{{efn|Moore is the fifth black U.S. state governor, following [[P. B. S. Pinchback]] of Louisiana, [[Douglas Wilder]] of Virginia, [[Deval Patrick]] of Massachusetts and [[David Paterson]] of New York. Pinchback and Paterson were not elected, but succeeded from the lieutenant governorship.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Milevski |first1=Laila |title=How many Black governors have served in the U.S. before Wes Moore? |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/article/black-governors-united-states-FQNH7JICC5DYHK274VCZCKWMUI/ |access-date=January 19, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=January 19, 2023}}</ref>}}<ref name="2022Win">{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=November 9, 2022 |title=Moore, a Democrat, Will Become Maryland's First Black Governor |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/08/us/politics/maryland-governor-wes-moore.html |access-date=November 9, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="history"/>

==Early life and education==
===Early life===
Moore was born on October 15, 1978, in [[Takoma Park, Maryland]], a suburb of [[Washington, D.C.|Washington D.C.]] His father, William Westley Moore Jr., was a broadcast news journalist,<ref>{{cite news |last1=May |first1=Eric Charles |date=December 17, 1987 |title=PEOPLE |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/local/1987/12/17/people/9e0dc420-5b6f-48f8-b2d1-946dffff4373/ |access-date=July 20, 2022}}</ref> and his mother, Joy Thomas Moore,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Excerpt from The Other Wes Moore |url=https://www.oprah.com/oprahshow/excerpt-from-the-other-wes-moore-by-wes-moore |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100501142605/https://www.oprah.com/oprahshow/excerpt-from-the-other-wes-moore-by-wes-moore |archive-date=May 1, 2010 |access-date=December 27, 2020 |website=Oprah.com}}</ref> is a daughter of immigrants from [[Cuba]] and [[Jamaica]], and a news media professional.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Joy Thomas Moore |url=https://maec.org/staff/joy-thomas-moore/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211213172714/https://maec.org/staff/joy-thomas-moore/ |archive-date=December 13, 2021 |access-date=December 27, 2020 |website=MAEC, Inc. |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Wes Moore for Maryland |url=https://wesmoore.com/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19991004041808/https://wesmoore.com/ |archive-date=October 4, 1999 |access-date=June 3, 2021 |website=Wes Moore for Maryland}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=About The Author |url=http://theotherwesmoore.com/about-the-author/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150715102812/http://theotherwesmoore.com/about-the-author/ |archive-date=July 15, 2015 |access-date=July 15, 2015 |website=The Other Wes Moore}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cassie |first1=Ron |title=Wes Moore to Become Maryland's First Black Governor |url=https://www.baltimoremagazine.com/section/historypolitics/wes-moore-to-become-marylands-first-black-governor/ |access-date=May 7, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore (magazine)|Baltimore]] |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref> His maternal grandfather, James Thomas, a Jamaican immigrant,<ref name="Character List2">{{Cite web |title=Character List |url=http://theotherwesmoore2.weebly.com/character-list.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141011193843/http://theotherwesmoore2.weebly.com/character-list.html |archive-date=October 11, 2014 |access-date=December 27, 2020 |website=The Other Wes Moore: One Name, Two Fates}}</ref> was the first black minister in the history of the [[Dutch Reformed Church]].<ref name="The Other Wes Moore3">{{cite book |last1=Moore |first1=Wes |url=https://archive.org/details/otherwesmooreone00moor_0/page/250 |title=The Other Wes Moore |date=January 11, 2011 |publisher=Random House Publishing Group |isbn=9780385528207 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/otherwesmooreone00moor_0/page/250 250] |access-date=August 18, 2015}}</ref> His grandmother, Winell Thomas, a Cuban who moved to Jamaica before immigrating to the U.S., was a retired schoolteacher.<ref name="Character List2" />

On April 15, 1982, when Moore was three years old,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hawkins |first=Beth |date=November 2, 2012 |title=The Wes Moores: two fatherless boys, 2 different paths |url=https://www.minnpost.com/learning-curve/2012/11/wes-moores-two-fatherless-boys-two-very-different-paths/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103235923/https://www.minnpost.com/learning-curve/2012/11/wes-moores-two-fatherless-boys-two-very-different-paths/ |archive-date=November 3, 2012 |access-date=December 27, 2020 |website=MinnPost |language=en-US}}</ref> his father died of acute [[epiglottitis]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cheng |first=Allen |date=October 7, 2020 |title=The Other Wes Moore Book Summary, by Wes Moore |url=https://www.allencheng.com/the-other-wes-moore-book-summary-wes-moore/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020123036/https://www.allencheng.com/the-other-wes-moore-book-summary-wes-moore/ |archive-date=October 20, 2022 |access-date=December 27, 2020 |website=Allen Cheng |language=en-US}}</ref> In the summer of 1984, Moore's mother took him and his two sisters to live in [[the Bronx]], New York, with her parents.<ref name="Character List2" /> His occasional [[Babysitting|babysitter]] was [[Kamala Harris]]'s stepmother, Carol Kirlew.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Draper |first=Robert |date=October 4, 2024 |title=Kamala Harris and the Influence of an Estranged Father Just Two Miles Away |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/10/04/us/politics/kamala-harris-father.html |access-date=October 4, 2024 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> Moore attended [[Riverdale Country School]]. When his grades declined and he became involved in [[petty crime]], his mother enrolled him in [[Valley Forge Military Academy|Valley Forge Military Academy and College]].<ref name="The Other Wes Moore3" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Trent |first1=Sydney |title=Wes Moore tried to run away from military school. It changed his life instead. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/11/02/wes-moore-military-school-valley-forge/ |access-date=November 2, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=November 2, 2022}}</ref> Moore's family moved back to Maryland after his mother's employer, the [[Annie E. Casey Foundation]], relocated to Baltimore.<ref name="Rhodes" />

In 1998, Moore graduated [[Phi Theta Kappa]] from [[Valley Forge Military College]] with an [[associate degree]]. He then attended [[Johns Hopkins University]], from which he graduated [[Phi Beta Kappa]] with a B.A. in [[international relations]] and economics in 2001.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 17, 2013 |title=Author, JHU alum Wes Moore to speak at School of Education commencement |url=http://hub.jhu.edu/2013/04/17/wes-moore-education-commencement/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130801042611/http://hub.jhu.edu/2013/04/17/wes-moore-education-commencement/ |archive-date=August 1, 2013 |access-date=October 11, 2016}}</ref> At Johns Hopkins, he also played wide receiver for the [[Johns Hopkins Blue Jays football]] team for two seasons,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lee |first1=Edward |title='The guy's got a way about him': Maryland Gov.-elect Wes Moore honed leadership skills as Johns Hopkins football player |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/12/15/the-guys-got-a-way-about-him-maryland-gov-elect-wes-moore-honed-leadership-skills-as-johns-hopkins-football-player/ |access-date=December 15, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=December 15, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Former JHU Football Player Wes Moore Selected as 2006-07 White House Fellow |url=https://hopkinssports.com/news/2006/6/21/former_jhu_football_player_wes_moore_selected_as_2006_07_white_house_fellow.aspx |website=hopkinssports.com |publisher=[[Johns Hopkins Blue Jays]] |access-date=November 13, 2022 |date=June 21, 2006}}</ref> served as the chair of the university's Men of the [[NAACP]] branch,<ref name="Rhodes">{{cite press release |title=Johns Hopkins Student Selected for Rhodes Scholarship |url=https://pages.jh.edu/news_info/news/home00/dec00/rhodes.html |access-date=October 8, 2025 |work=Headlines@Hopkins |publisher=[[Johns Hopkins University]] |date=December 10, 2000}}</ref> and was initiated into the [[Omicron Delta Kappa]] honor society, and Sigma Sigma chapter of [[Alpha Phi Alpha]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Maryland's New Governor, Wes Moore, Is a Brother of Alpha Phi Alpha |url=https://www.watchtheyard.com/alphas/marylands-new-governor-wes-moore-is-a-brother-of-alpha-phi-alpha/ |website=watchtheyard.com |publisher=Watch The Yard |access-date=November 13, 2022 |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref>

In 1998 and 1999, Moore interned for [[Baltimore Mayor]] [[Kurt Schmoke]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cadiz |first1=Laura |title=Hopkins senior a Rhodes scholar |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2000/12/11/hopkins-senior-a-rhodes-scholar-2/ |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=December 11, 2000 |archive-date=June 3, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012448/https://www.baltimoresun.com/entertainment/bs-xpm-2000-12-11-bal-moore-jhu-rhodes-0430-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref> He later became involved with the [[March of Dimes]] before serving in the Army.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Strauss |first1=Valerie |title=Payoff on a Parent's Persistence |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/2001/01/23/payoff-on-a-parents-persistence/705b3e66-d1d6-416f-893f-fe2950c551a8/ |access-date=September 18, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=January 23, 2001}}</ref> He also interned at the [[United States Department of Homeland Security]] under Secretary [[Tom Ridge]].<ref name="Fellow" />

After graduating, he attended [[Wolfson College, Oxford]] as a [[Rhodes Scholar]], where he earned a master's degree in international relations in 2004<ref>{{cite news |last1=Harris |first1=Elizabeth |date=April 25, 2017 |title=Robin Hood, Favorite Charity on Wall Street, Gets New Leader |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/25/nyregion/robin-hood-foundation-charity-wes-moore.html |url-status=live |access-date=September 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220928140709/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/25/nyregion/robin-hood-foundation-charity-wes-moore.html |archive-date=September 28, 2022}}</ref> and submitted a thesis titled ''Rise and Ramifications of Radical Islam in the Western Hemisphere''.<ref name="WhoIsMoore" /> While at Oxford, Moore shared a house with [[Luke Bronin]].<ref name="ComesFrom" />

===Military service===
After graduating from Valley Forge, Moore completed the requirements for the [[United States Army]]'s early commissioning program, and was appointed a [[Second lieutenant (United States)|second lieutenant]] of [[Military Intelligence Corps (United States Army)|Military Intelligence]] in the [[United States Army Reserve|Army Reserve]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Wes Moore |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2022/12/30/1-wes-moore/ |access-date=June 11, 2026 |work=The Daily Record |date=December 30, 2022}}</ref> In January 2003, the Army ordered Moore to report to [[Fort Benning]] to complete infantry-officer basic training required for deployment to [[Afghanistan]], during which he aggravated a knee injury suffered in college and could not finish the training. He arrived at [[Fort Leonard Wood]] in February 2005 to complete his training.<ref name="MilitaryCareer" />

Moore served in the [[82nd Airborne Division]] and was deployed to Afghanistan from 2005 to March 2006,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Weisz |first1=Zac |title=Wes Moore has a plan |url=https://www.nationaljournal.com/s/719378/wes-moore-has-a-plan/ |access-date=November 1, 2022 |work=[[National Journal]] |date=November 1, 2022}}</ref><ref name="MilitaryCareer" /> starting as an information operations officer<ref name="MilitaryCareer" /> and later attaining the rank of [[Captain (United States O-3)|captain]].<ref name="leaving3" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Rogers |first1=Keith |date=April 27, 2014 |title=Author to screen his PBS documentary on returning veterans |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]] |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/military/author-to-screen-his-pbs-documentary-on-returning-veterans/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020123041/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/military/author-to-screen-his-pbs-documentary-on-returning-veterans/ |archive-date=October 20, 2022}}</ref> During his service, he was awarded the [[Afghanistan Campaign Medal]], [[Armed Forces Reserve Medal]] with "M" devices, [[Army Service Ribbon]], [[Bronze Star Medal]], [[Combat Action Badge]], [[National Defense Service Medal]], and the [[Parachutist Badge (United States)|Parachutist Badge]].<ref name="WhoIsMoore" /><ref name="BronzeStar" /><ref name=WaPo/> Moore left the Army in 2014.<ref name="WhoIsMoore">{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Who is Maryland's next governor, Wes Moore? |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/who-is-marylands-next-governor-wes-moore-4I4S3RHJJJCUHBY3IUZO7ANWPM/ |access-date=November 9, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref>

==Career==
[[File:Social Impact Discussion with Wes Moore (49596383388).jpg|thumb|225px|Moore at Social Innovation Summit<br/>by [[New America (organization)|New America]] in January 2020]]
In February 2006, Moore was named a [[White House Fellows|White House Fellow]] to Secretary of State [[Condoleezza Rice]].<ref name="leaving3" /><ref>{{cite news |date=February 27, 2006 |title=The White House Announces Regional Finalists for the 2006-2007 White House Fellowships |work=[[The White House]] |url=https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/news/releases/2006/02/20060227-2.html |url-status=live |access-date=September 5, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220905202728/https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/news/releases/2006/02/20060227-2.html |archive-date=September 5, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Class of 2006-2007 |url=https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/fellows/alumni/2006-07.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826173210/https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/fellows/alumni/2006-07.html |archive-date=August 26, 2022 |access-date=September 5, 2022 |website=White House Fellows |publisher=[[The White House]]}}</ref> He later worked as an investment banker at [[Deutsche Bank]] in [[Manhattan]]<ref name="Fellow">{{cite news |last1=Davis |first1=Julie Hirschfeld |date=July 3, 2006 |title=Path leads city man to halls of power |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2006/07/03/path-leads-city-man-to-halls-of-power/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920172841/https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/bs-xpm-2006-07-03-0607030015-story.html |archive-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> and at [[Citibank]] from 2007 to 2012<ref name="Need to know">{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=What you need to know about Maryland Democratic gubernatorial nominee Wes Moore |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/07/24/what-you-need-to-know-about-maryland-democratic-gubernatorial-nominee-wes-moore/ |access-date=July 23, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 23, 2022 |archive-date=July 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220723194836/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-who-is-wes-moore-20220723-ii3osqsse5hxxn23ai4msycs7q-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref> while living in [[Jersey City, New Jersey]].<ref name="leaving3" /><ref name="Circling2">{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |date=October 8, 2022 |title=After a lifetime of circling politics, Wes Moore picks his moment. Will Maryland voters hire him for his most ambitious job yet? |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/10/08/after-a-lifetime-of-circling-politics-wes-moore-picks-his-moment-will-maryland-voters-hire-him-for-his-most-ambitious-job-yet/ |url-status=live |access-date=October 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221008135229/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/elections/bs-md-pol-wes-moore-profile-governor-20221008-3jn6rzgqpjbxhhgnxx2b7746qy-story.html |archive-date=October 8, 2022}}</ref> In 2009, Moore was included on [[Crain’s New York Business]]'s "40 Under 40" list.<ref>{{cite web |date=July 26, 2018 |title=40 Under 40 Class of 2009 |url=https://www.crainsnewyork.com/awards/40-under-40-class-2009 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220928140719/https://www.crainsnewyork.com/awards/40-under-40-class-2009 |archive-date=September 28, 2022 |access-date=September 28, 2022 |website=crainsnewyork.com |publisher=[[Crain’s New York Business]]}}</ref>

In 2010, Moore founded a television production company, Omari Productions, to create content for networks such as the Oprah Winfrey Network, PBS, [[HBO]], and [[NBC]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Messner |first1=Rebecca |date=December 11, 2012 |title=Back in Baltimore, Wes Moore has big plans for his hometown |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2012/12/11/back-in-baltimore-wes-moore-has-big-plans-for-his-hometown/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012444/https://www.baltimoresun.com/entertainment/bthesite/bs-b-1212-wes-moore-20121210-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref> In May 2014, he produced a three-part PBS series, ''Coming Back with Wes Moore'', which followed the lives and experiences of returning veterans.<ref>{{cite web |title=Coming Back with Wes Moore |url=https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/american-veteran/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140529091941/https://www.pbs.org/veterans/stories-of-service/stream-tv/a-to-z/coming-back-with-wes-moore/ |archive-date=May 29, 2014 |access-date=June 2, 2022 |website=pbs.org |publisher=[[PBS]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Peterson |first1=Tyler |date=August 6, 2013 |title=PBS Orders COMING BACK WITH WES MOORE Veterans Special |work=[[Broadway World]] |url=https://www.broadwayworld.com/bwwtv/article/PBS-Orders-COMING-BACK-WITH-WES-MOORE-Veterans-Special-20130806 |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012440/https://www.broadwayworld.com/bwwtv/article/PBS-Orders-COMING-BACK-WITH-WES-MOORE-Veterans-Special-20130806 |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Zurawik |first1=David |date=May 9, 2014 |title='Coming Back' - At last, TV does right by veterans |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2014/05/09/coming-back-at-last-tv-does-right-by-veterans/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012450/https://www.baltimoresun.com/opinion/columnists/zurawik/bal-wes-moore-coming-back-veterans-20140509-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref>

In 2014, Moore founded BridgeEdU, a company that provided services to support students in their transition to college.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Herbst |first1=Diane |date=June 29, 2017 |title=The Improbable Life of Wes Moore, the New CEO of The Robin Hood Foundation: 'We Are Not Promised Anything' |work=[[People (magazine)|People]] |url=https://people.com/human-interest/wes-moore-ceo-robin-hood-foundation/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920172834/https://people.com/human-interest/wes-moore-ceo-robin-hood-foundation/ |archive-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> Students participating in BridgeEdU paid $500 into the program with varying fees.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gantz |first1=Sarah |date=June 15, 2015 |title=Wes Moore wants to help more students succeed in college |work=[[Baltimore Business Journal]] |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/baltimore/blog/cyberbizblog/2015/06/wes-moore-wants-to-help-more-students-succeed-in.html |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210420205715/https://www.bizjournals.com/baltimore/blog/cyberbizblog/2015/06/wes-moore-wants-to-help-more-students-succeed-in.html |archive-date=April 20, 2021}}</ref> BridgeEdU was not able to achieve financial stability and was acquired by student financial services company Edquity in 2019, mostly for its database of clients.<ref name="Under Armour2">{{cite news |last1=Mirabella |first1=Lorraine |date=September 3, 2020 |title=Wes Moore takes on director role at Under Armour |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2020/09/03/wes-moore-takes-on-director-role-at-under-armour/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920171544/https://www.baltimoresun.com/business/bs-bz-wes-moore-joins-under-armour-board-20200903-dyau6p6rsfabzgcp7giaf3ks3i-story.html |archive-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{cite news |last1=Bowie |first1=Liz |last2=Wood |first2=Pamela |date=May 3, 2022 |title=Wes Moore says his Baltimore education business was a success. The reality is much more complicated. |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/wes-moore-edu-company |url-status=live |access-date=May 21, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220504154038/https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/wes-moore-edu-company |archive-date=May 4, 2022}}</ref> A ''[[Baltimore Banner]]'' interview with former BridgeEdU students found that the short-lived company had mixed results.<ref name=":0" />

In September 2016, Moore produced ''All the Difference'', a PBS documentary that followed the lives of two young African-American men from the [[South Side of Chicago]] from high school through college and beyond.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Zurawik |first1=David |date=September 9, 2016 |title='All the Difference' tells new story of young black men in college |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2016/09/09/all-the-difference-tells-new-story-of-young-black-men-in-college/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012446/https://www.baltimoresun.com/opinion/columnists/zurawik/bs-ae-zontv-all-the-difference-pov-pbs-20160909-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=All the Difference {{!}} POV |url=https://www.pbs.org/pov/watch/allthedifference/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190909001521/https://www.pbs.org/pov/watch/allthedifference/ |archive-date=September 9, 2019 |access-date=June 2, 2022 |website=pbs.org |publisher=[[PBS]]}}</ref> Later that month, he launched ''Future City'', an interview-based talk show with Baltimore's [[WYPR]] station.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dunn |first1=Susan |date=September 19, 2016 |title=Wes Moore to Host Monthly Show on WYPR |work=Baltimore Fishbowl |url=https://baltimorefishbowl.com/stories/wes-moore-host-monthly-show-wypr/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108111332/https://baltimorefishbowl.com/stories/wes-moore-host-monthly-show-wypr/ |archive-date=November 8, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Future City |url=https://www.wypr.org/programs/future-city |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160917132814/https://www.wypr.org/programs/future-city |archive-date=September 17, 2016 |access-date=July 3, 2019 |website=www.wypr.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Britto |first1=Brittany |date=September 19, 2016 |title=Wes Moore to host monthly show on WYPR starting this week |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2016/09/19/wes-moore-to-host-monthly-show-on-wypr-starting-this-week/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012443/https://www.baltimoresun.com/features/baltimore-insider/bal-wes-moore-to-host-new-show-on-wypr-20160919-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref>

From June 2017 until May 2021, Moore was CEO of the [[Robin Hood Foundation]], a charitable organization that attempts to alleviate problems caused by poverty in New York City. It works mainly through funding schools, [[Food bank|food pantries]] and shelters. It also administers a disaster relief fund.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Wes Moore {{!}} Robin Hood |url=https://www.robinhood.org/wes-moore/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180103214454/https://www.robinhood.org/wes-moore/ |archive-date=January 3, 2018 |access-date=October 7, 2018 |website=robinhood.org |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid |date=July 16, 2022 |title=Unpredictable Maryland Governor's Race Pits Old Guard vs. Upstarts |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/16/us/politics/maryland-primary-tom-perez-wes-moore.html |url-status=live |access-date=July 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718003302/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/16/us/politics/maryland-primary-tom-perez-wes-moore.html |archive-date=July 18, 2022}}</ref><ref name="leaving3" /><ref>[https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/16/robin-hood-foundation-ceo-wes-moore-on-achieving-success.html CNBC profile] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718160540/https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/16/robin-hood-foundation-ceo-wes-moore-on-achieving-success.html|date=July 18, 2022}}, Robin Hood Foundation CEO Wes Moore: ‘Have faith, not fear. I feel that has guided me’, February 16, 2021</ref> During his tenure as CEO, the organization also raised more than $650 million, including $230 million in 2020 to provide increased need for assistance during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Deutch |first1=Gabby |date=October 18, 2021 |title=Wes Moore bets on Maryland |work=[[Jewish Insider]] |url=https://jewishinsider.com/2021/10/wes-moore-maryland-governor-democratic-primary/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920171030/https://jewishinsider.com/2021/10/wes-moore-maryland-governor-democratic-primary/ |archive-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> Moore also sought to expand his advocacy to include America's poor and transform the organization into a national force in the poverty fight.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gordon |first1=Amanda L. |title=Robin Hood CEO, Tina Fey, Gerwig Start New York's Awards Season |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-01-12/robin-hood-ceo-tina-fey-gerwig-start-new-york-s-awards-season |access-date=February 6, 2023 |work=[[Bloomberg News]] |date=January 12, 2018}}</ref>

Prior to his election as governor, Moore was a member of the boards of directors for [[Under Armour]] and [[Green Thumb Industries]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Neukam |first1=Stephen |title=Maryland gubernatorial candidate's financial connections pose conflict problems |url=https://cnsmaryland.org/2022/04/29/maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-financial-connections-pose-conflict-problems/ |access-date=May 21, 2022 |work=[[Capital News Service (Maryland)|Capital News Service]] |date=April 29, 2022 |archive-date=April 29, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220429162319/https://cnsmaryland.org/2022/04/29/maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-financial-connections-pose-conflict-problems/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Under Armour2" /><ref>{{cite press release |title=Maryland Governor-elect Wes Moore Steps Down From Under Armour's Board of Directors |url=https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/maryland-governor-elect-wes-moore-steps-down-from-under-armours-board-of-directors-301675197.html |access-date=November 11, 2022 |work=[[GlobeNewswire]] |date=November 11, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=Green Thumb Industries Announces Departure of Wes Moore from Board of Directors |url=https://www.globenewswire.com/en/news-release/2022/03/11/2401736/0/en/Green-Thumb-Industries-Announces-Departure-of-Wes-Moore-from-Board-of-Directors.html |access-date=October 28, 2022 |work=[[GlobeNewswire]] |date=March 11, 2022}}</ref> In October 2022, Moore announced that he would use a [[blind trust]] to hold his assets and resign from every board position if elected governor.<ref name="Finances">{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Dan Cox and Wes Moore won't release their tax returns in Maryland's gubernatorial race. Here's what's known about their finances. |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/10/28/dan-cox-and-wes-moore-wont-release-their-tax-returns-in-marylands-gubernatorial-race-heres-whats-known-about-their-finances/ |access-date=October 28, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=October 28, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Wes Moore says he'll hand control of his investments to a blind trust if elected governor |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/wes-moore-investments-SB2VJNYDGZAPVHWPOIBYDLYMQM/ |access-date=November 1, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=November 1, 2022}}</ref> In May 2023, Moore finalized his trust, making him the first governor to have one since [[Bob Ehrlich]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan P. |title=Moore puts millions into blind trust, will sell off major portion of cannabis holdings |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/05/01/moore-puts-millions-into-blind-trust-will-sell-off-major-portion-of-cannabis-holdings/ |access-date=May 1, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 1, 2023}}</ref> In May 2025, after similar conflict of interest concerns were raised about former governor [[Larry Hogan]] during his [[2024 United States Senate election in Maryland|2024 U.S. Senate campaign]], Moore signed into law a bill requiring future governors to put their assets into a blind trust or sign an agreement not to participate in decisions affecting their businesses.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=New ethics law clamps down on future Maryland governors' business dealings |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/maryland-governor-ethics-law-SAAQ7LPZ5RF6BPOMCKI72K43YY/ |access-date=May 6, 2025 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=May 6, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Books ===
On April 27, 2010, [[Spiegel & Grau]] published his first book, ''[[The Other Wes Moore]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rosenthal |first1=Dave |date=April 27, 2010 |title=The Other Wes Moore -- the two faces of Baltimore |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/bs-mtblog-2010-04-the_other_wes_moore_the_two_fa-story.html |url-status=dead |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920171116/https://www.baltimoresun.com/bs-mtblog-2010-04-the_other_wes_moore_the_two_fa-story.html |archive-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> The 200-page book explores the lives of two young Baltimore boys who shared the same name and race, but largely different familial histories that leads them both down very different paths.<ref name="The Other Wes Moore3" /><ref>{{cite magazine |title=The Other Wes Moore: One Name and Two Fates—A Story of Tragedy and Hope |url=https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-0-385-52819-1 |url-status=live |magazine=[[Publishers Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190318194500/https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-0-385-52819-1 |archive-date=March 18, 2019 |access-date=June 2, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sragow |first1=Michael |date=April 30, 2010 |title='The Other Wes Moore' tells a tale of two inner-city destinies |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2010/04/30/the-other-wes-moore-tells-a-tale-of-two-inner-city-destinies-2/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210621004626/https://www.baltimoresun.com/entertainment/bs-xpm-2010-04-30-bs-ae-wes-moore-20100430-21-story.html |archive-date=June 21, 2021}}</ref> In December 2012, Moore announced that ''The Other Wes Moore'' would be developed into a feature film, with [[Oprah Winfrey]] attached as an executive producer.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Messner |first1=Rebecca |date=December 11, 2012 |title=Oprah executive producing film adaptation of 'The Other Wes Moore' |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2012/12/11/oprah-executive-producing-film-adaptation-of-the-other-wes-moore/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012440/https://www.baltimoresun.com/entertainment/bthesite/bs-b-1212-wes-moore-film-20121211-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref> In September 2013, [[Random House|Ember]] published his second book, ''Discovering Wes Moore''. The book maintains the message and story set out in ''The Other Wes Moore'', but is more accessible to young adults.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Moore |first1=Wes |title=Discovering Wes Moore |url=http://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/212868/discovering-wes-moore-the-young-adult-adaptation-by-wes-moore/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924070839/http://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/212868/discovering-wes-moore-the-young-adult-adaptation-by-wes-moore/ |archive-date=September 24, 2015 |access-date=August 18, 2015 |website=Penguinrandomhouse.com |publisher=Penguin Random House}}</ref> In April 2021, [[Unanimous Media]] announced it would adapt ''The Other Wes Moore'' into a feature film.<ref>{{cite news |last1=D'Allesandro |first1=Anthony |date=April 27, 2021 |title=Unanimous Media & Pathways Alliance Arm Developing Feature Adaptation Of 'The Other Wes Moore' |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |url=https://deadline.com/2021/04/the-other-wes-moore-feature-adaptation-stephen-curry-producing-1234745296/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427222547/https://deadline.com/2021/04/the-other-wes-moore-feature-adaptation-stephen-curry-producing-1234745296/ |archive-date=April 27, 2021}}</ref> As of June 2022, a film has yet to be produced.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Swift |first1=Tim |date=May 31, 2022 |title=Oprah Winfrey, Maryland governor candidate Wes Moore to hold virtual fundraiser |work=[[WBFF]] |url=https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/oprah-winfrey-maryland-governor-candidate-wes-moore-to-hold-virtual-fundraiser |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220601233708/https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/oprah-winfrey-maryland-governor-candidate-wes-moore-to-hold-virtual-fundraiser |archive-date=June 1, 2022}}</ref>

In January 2015, Moore wrote his third book, ''The Work''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCauley |first1=Mary Carole |date=January 24, 2015 |title=Baltimore author Wes Moore publishes his second book |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2015/01/24/baltimore-author-wes-moore-publishes-his-second-book/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012452/https://www.baltimoresun.com/entertainment/arts/bs-ae-author-moore-20150124-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref> In November 2016, he wrote ''This Way Home'', a young adult novel about Elijah, a high school basketball player, who emerges from a standoff with a local gang after they attempt to recruit him to their basketball team, and he refuses.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Moore |first1=Wes |title=This Way Home |date=November 10, 2015 |publisher=Random House Childrens Books |isbn=978-0385741699 |pages=256}}</ref> In March 2020, Moore and former ''[[Baltimore Sun]]'' education reporter [[Erica L. Green]] wrote ''Five Days: The Fiery Reckoning of an American City'', which explores the [[2015 Baltimore protests]] from the perspectives of eight Baltimoreans who experienced it on the front lines.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Campbell |first1=Colin |date=June 28, 2020 |title=Wes Moore, others discuss underlying race issues, reforms and societal failures in virtual 'Five Days' panel |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2020/06/28/wes-moore-others-discuss-underlying-race-issues-reforms-and-societal-failures-in-virtual-five-days-panel/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012457/https://www.baltimoresun.com/maryland/baltimore-city/bs-md-ci-wes-moore-panel-20200629-sk4icrb24fcyhmg7kon5irjgme-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Greenhouse |first1=Lisa |date=September 16, 2020 |title=A Look at Wes Moore's new Book about the Baltimore Uprising "Five Days" |work=[[Enoch Pratt Free Library]] |url=https://blog.prattlibrary.org/2020/09/16/a-look-at-wes-moores-new-book-about-the-baltimore-uprising-five-days/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020123051/https://blog.prattlibrary.org/2020/09/16/a-look-at-wes-moores-new-book-about-the-baltimore-uprising-five-days/ |archive-date=October 20, 2022}}</ref>

===Disputed biographical claims===
In April 2022, [[CNN]] accused Moore of embellishing his childhood and where he actually grew up.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dovere |first1=Edward-Isaac |title=A rising Democratic star told his origin story. But did he allow a narrative to take hold that didn't match the facts? |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/13/politics/wes-moore-baltimore-origin-story-campaign-governor-maryland/index.html |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[CNN]] |date=April 13, 2022 |archive-date=June 9, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220609232622/https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/13/politics/wes-moore-baltimore-origin-story-campaign-governor-maryland/index.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Shortly after the article was published, Moore created a website that attempted to rebut the allegations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stole |first1=Bryn |title='I've been very clear and transparent,' Maryland gubernatorial candidate Wes Moore says about his Baltimore ties |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/04/15/ive-been-very-clear-and-transparent-maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-wes-moore-says-about-his-baltimore-ties/ |access-date=July 5, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=April 15, 2022 |archive-date=July 5, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220705222712/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-wes-moore-baltimore-20220416-wwqpjfmbbre6pogdr3qcrx3zyq-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Bronze Star Presentation (54223021150).jpg|thumb|225px|Lieutenant General [[Michael R. Fenzel]] presents Moore with a Bronze Star Medal, 2024]]
Lieutenant General [[Michael R. Fenzel]] recommended Moore for the Bronze Star in 2006. Moore applied to a White House fellowship that year. According to Moore and Frenzel, Fenzel encouraged Moore to say he'd received the Bronze Star in his fellowship application even though it was not yet awarded. Frenzel claimed that he assumed the medal would have been awarded by the time the fellows were named. By 2024, the Bronze Star had still not been awarded, when Fenzel resubmitted the paperwork for Moore to receive the [[Bronze Star Medal]]. On November 19, 2024, Moore was finally cited with a Bronze Star Medal for meritorious service in [[War in Afghanistan (2001–2021)|Afghanistan]]. Lieutenant General Fenzel pinned the decoration on Moore on December 14. Fenzel stated that Moore's medal was awarded 20 years late.<ref name=WaPo>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |title=Eighteen years and one controversy later, Wes Moore gets a Bronze Star |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/12/21/wes-moore-bronze-star/ |access-date=August 28, 2025 |work=The Washington Post |date=December 21, 2024}}</ref> Moore failed to correct journalists who referred to him as a Bronze Star recipient, and he apologized for the mistake.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Frost |first1=Mikenzie |title=Bronze Star recipient? Wes Moore seen failing to correct record again in past interview |url=https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/bronze-star-recipient-wes-moore-appears-to-misrepresent-military-record-in-past-interview |access-date=April 29, 2022 |work=[[WBFF]] |date=April 28, 2022 |archive-date=April 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220428212309/https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/bronze-star-recipient-wes-moore-appears-to-misrepresent-military-record-in-past-interview |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Stole |first1=Bryn |title=Maryland's Wes Moore pushes back against criticism he failed to set interviewers straight about his background |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/04/29/marylands-wes-moore-pushes-back-against-criticism-he-failed-to-set-interviewers-straight-about-his-background/ |access-date=April 29, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=April 29, 2022 |archive-date=April 29, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220429231148/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-wes-moore-20220429-4m55go723nggzaf4a5fhl3ixv4-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BronzeStar">{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid J. |title=Wes Moore and the Bronze Star He Claimed but Never Received |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/29/us/politics/wes-moore-bronze-star.html |access-date=August 29, 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=August 29, 2024}}</ref>

In April 2026, ''[[Semafor (website)|Semafor]]'' and ''[[Politico]]'' reported that [[David D. Smith]], the executive chairman of [[Sinclair Broadcast Group]] and owner of ''[[The Baltimore Sun]]'', was "deeply involved" with efforts to investigate Moore's military record, with ''The Sun'' having brought on a team of investigators from [[WBFF|Fox 45]] to comb through his military and collegiate athletic records.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Blanchard |first1=Jack |last2=Burns |first2=Dasha |title=The two sides of American power |url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook/2026/04/06/the-two-sides-of-american-power-00859474 |access-date=June 11, 2026 |work=[[Politico]] |date=April 6, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> In June 2026, Moore's staff provided ''[[The Baltimore Banner]]'' with access to his military records and helped coordinate interviews with people who served with Moore or knew details of his deployment. In their review, ''The Banner'' found that Moore was a competent, well-respected soldier, but mischaracterized or inaccurately recounted details about his military record in his public statements and writings about his tenure. Moore acknowledged these mischaracterizations, but added that his errors should not be construed in a way that would undermine his military service.<ref name="MilitaryCareer">{{cite news |last1=Sanderlin |first1=Lee O. |title=Wes Moore's military service is not in doubt. His storytelling is. |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/maryland-wes-moore-war-military-history-scrutiny-XVR6V4P5NRCZFJJSGK22JG5TFE/ |access-date=June 11, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=June 11, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

== Political career ==

=== Ideology ===
During an August 2006 interview with [[C-SPAN]], Moore said he identified as a "registered Democrat" who is "social moderate and strong fiscal conservative".<ref>{{cite web |date=August 25, 2006 |title=Q&A with Westley Moore |url=https://www.c-span.org/video/?194040-1%2Fqa-westley-moore |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141109043943/https://www.c-span.org/video/?194040-1%2Fqa-westley-moore |archive-date=November 9, 2014 |access-date=July 15, 2022 |website=c-span.org |publisher=[[C-SPAN]] |quote=You know I look at my history and I look at the fact that I am, you know, I'm a social moderate. I'm a, you know, strong fiscal conservative. I'm a military officer. I'm an investment banker and I just happen to be also a registered Democrat.}}</ref> In September 2022, he reiterated his position on fiscal issues as being "fiscally responsible".<ref name="Charisma">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |date=September 18, 2022 |title=Charisma fueled Wes Moore's primary win. Now he sharpens his focus on policy. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/18/wes-moore-governor-poverty-platform/ |access-date=September 18, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> During his gubernatorial campaign, he was described as center-left<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |date=September 19, 2022 |title=Poll: Wes Moore leads big against Dan Cox in Md. governor's race |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/19/poll-moore-leads-cox/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220919190614/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/19/poll-moore-leads-cox/ |archive-date=September 19, 2022 |access-date=September 27, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> as well as [[Progressivism in the United States|progressive]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |date=July 18, 2022 |title=Five questions ahead of Maryland's vacation time primary election |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/07/18/five-questions-ahead-of-marylands-vacation-time-primary-election/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718111637/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-five-things-primary-2022-20220718-joygk2dnj5djjnjwi7kzjx6qye-story.html |archive-date=July 18, 2022 |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref> He has been described as a [[Political moderate|moderate]] during his tenure as governor.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Peck |first1=Louis |date=July 18, 2023 |title=The Almanac of American Politics on Moore: A charismatic leader who broke barriers as a political outsider |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2023/07/18/the-almanac-of-american-politics-on-moore-a-charismatic-leader-who-broke-barriers-as-a-political-outsider/ |access-date=April 10, 2025 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref><ref name="history">{{cite news |last1=Booker |first1=Brakkton |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Wes Moore makes history as Maryland's first Black governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/08/maryland-governor-results-2022-wes-moore-00065789 |access-date=April 10, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |language=en}}</ref>

Moore has cited [[Jared Polis]], [[Parris Glendening]], and [[Roy Cooper]] as his political role models.<ref name="Glendening"/><ref name="BulwarkMoore">{{cite news |last1=Miller |first1=Tim |last2=Swift |first2=Jim |date=September 27, 2022 |title=Can Wes Moore's Progressive Patriotism Make Him a Democratic Star? |url=https://www.thebulwark.com/wes-moore-and-the-challenge-of-progressive-patriotism/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220927093118/https://www.thebulwark.com/wes-moore-and-the-challenge-of-progressive-patriotism/ |archive-date=September 27, 2022 |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[The Bulwark (website)|The Bulwark]]}}</ref>

===Early political involvement===
Moore first expressed interest in politics in June 1996, telling a ''[[The New York Times|New York Times]]'' reporter that he planned to attend law school and enter politics after two years at Valley Forge.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rhoden |first1=William |date=June 28, 1996 |title=ON BASKETBALL;No Longer Trapped by the Stuff Dreams Are Made Of |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/06/28/sports/on-basketball-no-longer-trapped-by-the-stuff-dreams-are-made-of.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221008133659/https://www.nytimes.com/1996/06/28/sports/on-basketball-no-longer-trapped-by-the-stuff-dreams-are-made-of.html |archive-date=October 8, 2022 |access-date=October 8, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> Moore first expressed interest in running for governor of Maryland during an interview with ''[[The Baltimore Sun]]'' in 2006,<ref name="Fellow" /> later telling ''The Baltimore Sun'' in October 2022 that he felt the idea of holding elected office only started to feel like a real possibility in 2020, when he was about to leave his job running Robin Hood.<ref name="Circling2" />
[[File:2014-20-2014_MDP-6625.jpg|thumb|225px|Moore (center) at a<br />[[Maryland Democratic Party]] picnic, 2014]]
Moore gave a speech at the [[2008 Democratic National Convention]], supporting [[Barack Obama]] for president.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mulcahy |first1=Conrad |date=August 29, 2008 |title=THE CAUCUS; Denver Brigade |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage-9500E6DD1038F93AA1575BC0A96E9C8B63.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220928140711/https://archive.nytimes.com/query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage-9500E6DD1038F93AA1575BC0A96E9C8B63.html |archive-date=September 28, 2022 |access-date=September 28, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=August 28, 2008 |title=2008 Democratic Convention, Day 4 |url=https://www.c-span.org/video/?280566-1%2F2008-democratic-convention |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220928140711/https://www.c-span.org/video/?280566-1%2F2008-democratic-convention |archive-date=September 28, 2022 |access-date=September 28, 2022 |website=c-span.org |publisher=[[C-SPAN]]}}</ref> In 2013, he said that he had "no interest" in running for public office, instead focusing on his business and volunteer work.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Broadwater |first1=Luke |date=June 9, 2013 |title=What's next for Wes Moore? |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2013/06/09/whats-next-for-wes-moore-3/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012442/https://www.baltimoresun.com/maryland/baltimore-city/bs-xpm-2013-06-09-bs-md-ci-wes-moore-20130609-story.html |archive-date=June 3, 2022 |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref> Later that year, [[Attorney General of Maryland|Attorney General]] [[Doug Gansler]] said that he considered choosing Moore as his running mate in the [[2014 Maryland gubernatorial election]], in which he ran with [[Maryland House of Delegates|state delegate]] [[Jolene Ivey]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wagner |first1=John |date=October 11, 2013 |title=Gansler to announce Jolene Ivey as running mate in Maryland's race for governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/gansler-to-announce-jolene-ivey-as-running-mate-in-marylands-race-for-governor/2013/10/11/76bb1f98-328b-11e3-8627-c5d7de0a046b_story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404145105/http://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/gansler-to-announce-jolene-ivey-as-running-mate-in-marylands-race-for-governor/2013/10/11/76bb1f98-328b-11e3-8627-c5d7de0a046b_story.html |archive-date=April 4, 2015 |access-date=June 2, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref>

In April 2015, following the [[2015 Baltimore protests]], Moore said that the demonstrations in Baltimore were a "long time coming"<ref>{{cite news |date=April 28, 2015 |title=Wes Moore: Demonstrations a long time coming |url=https://www.msnbc.com/morning-joe/watch/wes-moore--demonstrations-a-long-time-coming-435572803898 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150919110335/http://www.msnbc.com/morning-joe/watch/wes-moore--demonstrations-a-long-time-coming-435572803898 |archive-date=September 19, 2015 |access-date=September 18, 2015 |work=[[MSNBC]]}}</ref> and that Baltimore "must seize this moment to redress systemic problems and grow."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marbella |first1=Jean |last2=Scharper |first2=Julie |date=April 29, 2015 |title=After Baltimore riots, fighting an image that paints a city 'with no control over itself' |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2015/04/29/after-baltimore-riots-fighting-an-image-that-paints-a-city-with-no-control-over-itself/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920172239/https://www.baltimoresun.com/maryland/baltimore-city/bs-ae-freddie-gray-image-20150429-story.html |archive-date=September 20, 2022 |access-date=September 18, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref> Moore attended the funeral for [[Freddie Gray]] but left early to catch a plane to Boston for a speech he was giving on urban poverty. He later said he "felt guilty being away, but it wasn't just that. An audience in Boston would listen to me talk about poverty, but at a historic moment in my own city's history, I was {{Abbr|MIA|missing in action}}."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burg |first1=Daniel Cotzin |date=August 11, 2020 |title=Memories of Freddie Gray and those Fiery 'Five Days' of Reckoning in Baltimore |url=https://jmoreliving.com/2020/08/11/memories-of-freddie-gray-and-those-fiery-five-days-of-reckoning-in-baltimore/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920173722/https://jmoreliving.com/2020/08/11/memories-of-freddie-gray-and-those-fiery-five-days-of-reckoning-in-baltimore/ |archive-date=September 20, 2022 |access-date=September 18, 2022 |work=JMORE Baltimore Jewish Living}}</ref> On the eighth anniversary of Gray's death in April 2023, Moore made a tweet calling his death a turning point for not just those who knew Gray personally, but the entire city.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sullivan |first1=Emily |last2=Wintrode |first2=Brenda |date=April 22, 2023 |title=Banner political notes: Unions unite; New Montgomery delegate; Baltimore police union vs. Moore |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/banner-political-notes-5S75UMXOQZFTZAXYKVEYBE2BBI/ |access-date=April 22, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]]}}</ref>

In February 2017, Governor [[Larry Hogan]] nominated Moore to serve on the [[University System of Maryland]] Board of Regents.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |date=February 17, 2017 |title=Baltimore author Wes Moore nominated to University System of Maryland board |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2017/02/17/baltimore-author-wes-moore-nominated-to-university-system-of-maryland-board/ |access-date=November 6, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref> In October 2020, Moore was named to serve on the transition team of [[Mayor of Baltimore|Baltimore mayor-elect]] [[Brandon Scott]].<ref>{{cite news |date=October 20, 2020 |title=Mayoral candidate Brandon Scott names civic, business and community leaders to transition team |url=https://baltimorefishbowl.com/stories/mayoral-candidate-brandon-scott-names-civic-business-and-community-leaders-to-transition-team/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220923213307/https://baltimorefishbowl.com/stories/mayoral-candidate-brandon-scott-names-civic-business-and-community-leaders-to-transition-team/ |archive-date=September 23, 2022 |access-date=September 23, 2022 |work=Baltimore Fishbowl}}</ref> In January 2021, Speaker of the Maryland House of Delegates [[Adrienne A. Jones]] consulted with Moore to craft her "black agenda" to tackle [[racial inequalities]] in housing, health, banking, government, and private corporations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wiggins |first1=Ovetta |date=January 19, 2021 |title=Maryland House speaker to unveil a 'Black agenda' focused on health, wealth, homeownership |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/maryland-speaker-black-agenda-/2021/01/18/ac1a9be8-5676-11eb-a817-e5e7f8a406d6_story.html |access-date=February 6, 2023 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref>

=== Gubernatorial campaigns ===

==== 2022 ====
{{Main|2022 Maryland gubernatorial election}}

[[File:Wes Moore at Clubs of Color (52449917661).jpg|thumb|225px|alt=Wes Moore and Aruna Miller stand in front of a campaign bus with a crowd of supporters|Moore campaigning in October 2022]]
In February 2021, Moore announced he was considering a run for [[governor of Maryland]] in the [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election|2022 election]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=Wes Moore Actively Exploring 2022 Bid for Governor |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/02/24/wes-moore-actively-exploring-2022-bid-for-governor/ |access-date=November 20, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 24, 2021 |archive-date=November 20, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211120214259/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/02/24/wes-moore-actively-exploring-2022-bid-for-governor/ |url-status=live}}</ref> He launched his campaign on June 7, 2021,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stole |first=Bryn |date=June 7, 2021 |title=Wes Moore, author and former nonprofit executive, launches campaign for Maryland governor |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2021/06/07/wes-moore-author-and-former-nonprofit-executive-launches-campaign-for-maryland-governor/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210607194937/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-wes-moore-20210607-tauzvx7rarflfkxpcbxy4x72ru-story.html |archive-date=June 7, 2021 |access-date=June 7, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wiggins |first1=Ovetta |date=June 7, 2021 |title=Author, former nonprofit leader Wes Moore launches bid for Maryland governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/wes-moore-maryland-governor/2021/06/07/c9526650-c796-11eb-a11b-6c6191ccd599_story.html |access-date=November 20, 2021}}</ref> emphasizing "work, wages, and wealth"<ref name="ComesFrom">{{cite magazine |last1=Ball |first1=Molly |date=February 14, 2023 |title=Where Wes Moore Comes From |url=https://time.com/6250784/wes-moore-interview/ |access-date=February 14, 2023 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|TIME]] |location=[[Easton, Maryland]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle |date=August 26, 2021 |title=Wes Moore: Work, Wages and Wealth Will be North Stars |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/08/26/wes-moore-work-wages-and-wealth-will-be-north-stars/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220705222715/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/08/26/wes-moore-work-wages-and-wealth-will-be-north-stars/ |archive-date=July 5, 2022 |access-date=July 5, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> and running on the slogan "leave no one behind".<ref name="CNS MooreCox">{{cite news |last1=Dashniell |first1=Timothy |last2=Gartner |first2=Emmett |date=September 16, 2022 |title=Cox, Moore campaigns heat up as early voting nears |url=https://cnsmaryland.org/2022/09/16/cox-and-moore-campaigns-heat-up-as-early-voting-nears/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220917012014/https://cnsmaryland.org/2022/09/16/cox-and-moore-campaigns-heat-up-as-early-voting-nears/ |archive-date=September 17, 2022 |access-date=September 17, 2022 |work=[[Capital News Service (Maryland)|Capital News Service]]}}</ref><ref name="DeployingCampaign">{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |date=September 6, 2022 |title=How Wes Moore is deploying his military service on the campaign trail |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/09/06/how-wes-moore-is-deploying-his-military-service-on-the-campaign-trail/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220906110237/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/09/06/how-wes-moore-is-deploying-his-military-service-on-the-campaign-trail/ |archive-date=September 6, 2022 |access-date=September 17, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> His running mate was [[Aruna Miller]], a former [[Maryland House of Delegates|state delegate]] who represented [[Maryland Legislative District 15|Maryland's 15th district]] from 2010 to 2019.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |date=December 9, 2021 |title=Moore Picks Ex-Delegate Aruna Miller to Be His Running Mate |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/12/09/moore-picks-ex-delegate-aruna-miller-to-be-his-running-mate/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220516044921/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/12/09/moore-picks-ex-delegate-aruna-miller-to-be-his-running-mate/ |archive-date=May 16, 2022 |access-date=April 7, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref>

During the primary, Moore was endorsed by House Majority Leader [[Steny Hoyer]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Montellaro |first1=Zach |date=April 28, 2022 |title=Hoyer endorses Moore in Maryland governor race |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/04/28/hoyer-wes-moore-endorse-maryland-governor-00028745 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220520115644/https://www.politico.com/news/2022/04/28/hoyer-wes-moore-endorse-maryland-governor-00028745 |archive-date=May 20, 2022 |access-date=May 21, 2022 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref> [[Prince George's County, Maryland|Prince George's County]] executive [[Angela Alsobrooks]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wiggins |first1=Ovetta |date=March 5, 2022 |title=Prince George's County Executive Alsobrooks endorses Wes Moore for Maryland governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/03/05/alsobrooks-moore-endorse-maryland-governor/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220313103501/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/03/05/alsobrooks-moore-endorse-maryland-governor/ |archive-date=March 13, 2022 |access-date=April 7, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> television host [[Oprah Winfrey]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |last2=Kurtz |first2=Josh |date=May 31, 2022 |title=Political Notes: Moore Getting the Oprah Treatment, Schulz Sticks to the Script, and Gansler Lays Out Crime Plan |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/05/31/political-notes-moore-getting-the-oprah-treatment-schulz-sticks-to-the-script-and-gansler-lays-out-crime-plan/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220602070240/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/05/31/political-notes-moore-getting-the-oprah-treatment-schulz-sticks-to-the-script-and-gansler-lays-out-crime-plan/ |archive-date=June 2, 2022 |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> and former Governor [[Parris Glendening]].<ref name="Glendening">{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |date=December 15, 2021 |title=Glendening Backs Moore in Democratic Race for Governor |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/12/15/glendening-backs-moore-in-democratic-race-for-governor/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220409201830/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/12/15/glendening-backs-moore-in-democratic-race-for-governor/ |archive-date=April 9, 2022 |access-date=April 7, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> He also received backing from the [[National Education Association|Maryland State Education Association]]<ref name="MSEA">{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |date=April 2, 2022 |title=Wes Moore Nabs Coveted State Teachers' Union Endorsement |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/04/02/wes-moore-nabs-coveted-state-teachers-union-endorsement/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405100703/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/04/02/wes-moore-nabs-coveted-state-teachers-union-endorsement/ |archive-date=April 5, 2022 |access-date=April 7, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> and [[VoteVets.org]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |date=September 15, 2021 |title=Veterans' Political Group Backs Moore for Governor |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/09/15/veterans-political-group-backs-moore-for-governor/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220516023907/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/09/15/veterans-political-group-backs-moore-for-governor/ |archive-date=May 16, 2022 |access-date=April 7, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref>

On April 6, 2022, Moore filed a complaint with the Maryland State Board of Elections against the gubernatorial campaign of [[John King Jr.]], accusing "an unidentified party" of anonymously disseminating "false and disparaging information regarding Wes Moore via electronic mail and social media in an orchestrated attempt to disparage Mr. Moore and damage his candidacy." The complaint also suggested that King "may be responsible for this smear campaign", which the King campaign denied.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |date=April 6, 2022 |title=Moore Campaign Files Complaint, Accuses King Campaign of Circulating False Information |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/04/06/moore-campaign-files-complaint-accuses-king-campaign-of-circulating-false-information/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220927143357/https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/04/06/moore-campaign-files-complaint-accuses-king-campaign-of-circulating-false-information/ |archive-date=September 27, 2022 |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |date=April 6, 2022 |title=Anonymous accusations about Wes Moore's Baltimore ties spark complaint in Maryland governor's race |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/anonymous-accusations-about-wes-moores-baltimore-ties-spark-complaint-in-maryland-governors-race-3PFCPJM6YJFDRPS22N24FE22Y4/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220927143358/https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/anonymous-accusations-about-wes-moores-baltimore-ties-spark-complaint-in-maryland-governors-race-3PFCPJM6YJFDRPS22N24FE22Y4/ |archive-date=September 27, 2022 |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]]}}</ref> In April 2024, King's campaign was fined $2,000 after prosecutors connected the email address to an [[IP address]] used by Joseph O'Hearn, King's campaign manager.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wood |first=Pamela |date=April 4, 2024 |title=State fines former governor candidate John King $2K over anonymous Moore attacks |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/john-king-campaign-fine-THT7JTGKWNFW3GOYDZPAHMCPZI/ |access-date=April 4, 2024 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]]}}</ref>

Moore won the Democratic primary on July 19, 2022, defeating former [[Democratic National Committee]] chairman [[Tom Perez]] and Comptroller [[Peter Franchot]] with 32.4% of the vote.<ref name="Nominee">{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid |date=July 22, 2022 |title=Wes Moore Wins the Democratic Primary for Maryland Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/22/us/politics/wes-moore-maryland.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220723032006/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/22/us/politics/wes-moore-maryland.html |archive-date=July 23, 2022 |access-date=October 22, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> During the general election, Moore twice campaigned with U.S. President [[Joe Biden]].<ref name="Biden">{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |date=August 25, 2022 |title=Biden rallies Maryland Democrats and stumps for Wes Moore in Montgomery County |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/biden-rallies-maryland-democrats-and-stumps-for-wes-moore-in-montgomery-county-DTBCKHZP2NHI5IRQB6UREPXGLM/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826003113/https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/biden-rallies-maryland-democrats-and-stumps-for-wes-moore-in-montgomery-county-DTBCKHZP2NHI5IRQB6UREPXGLM/ |archive-date=August 26, 2022 |access-date=August 25, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ford |first1=William J. |date=November 7, 2022 |title=Joe Biden Stumps for Wes Moore in pre-Election Day rally at Bowie State University |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/11/07/joe-biden-stumps-for-wes-moore-in-pre-election-day-rally-at-bowie-state-university/ |access-date=November 8, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]]}}</ref> He also campaigned on reclaiming "patriotism" from Republicans, highlighting his service in the U.S. Army while also bringing attention to [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee and state delegate [[Dan Cox]]'s participation in the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Soellner |first1=Mica |date=September 9, 2022 |title=Wes Moore runs on patriotism to take back Maryland governor's mansion for Democrats |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2022/sep/9/wes-moore-runs-patriotism-take-back-maryland-gover/ |access-date=December 17, 2022 |work=[[The Washington Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Dorman |first1=John L. |date=October 29, 2022 |title=Maryland Democratic gubernatorial nominee Wes Moore says MAGA can't 'define what it means to be a patriot' |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/wes-moore-maryland-governor-election-patriotism-democrats-maga-trump-2022-10 |access-date=December 17, 2022 |work=[[Business Insider]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McCammond |first1=Alexi |date=December 12, 2022 |title=Democrats aim to steal GOP playbook on patriotism and freedom |url=https://www.axios.com/2022/12/12/wes-moore-josh-shapiro-patriotism-freedom |access-date=December 17, 2022 |work=[[Axios (website)|Axios]]}}</ref> Moore defeated Cox in the general election,<ref name="2022Win" /> and became Maryland's first black governor<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shepard |first1=Ryan |date=June 8, 2021 |title=Wes Moore Strives To Become The First Black Governor Of Maryland |url=https://www.binnews.com/content/2021-06-08-wes-moore-strives-to-become-the-first-black-governor-of-maryland/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020123027/https://www.binnews.com/content/2021-06-08-wes-moore-strives-to-become-the-first-black-governor-of-maryland/ |archive-date=October 20, 2022 |access-date=July 15, 2022 |work=[[Black Information Network]]}}</ref> and the first veteran to be elected governor since [[William Donald Schaefer]].<ref name="DeployingCampaign" />

In December 2022, Moore was elected to serve as finance chair of the [[Democratic Governors Association]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Montellaro |first1=Zach |date=December 7, 2022 |title=Democrats elected a big class of young governors. They might be the future of the party. |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/12/07/democrats-young-governors-00072586 |access-date=December 8, 2022 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref>

==== 2026 ====
{{main|2026 Maryland gubernatorial election}}

On September 9, 2025, Moore announced that he would run for re-election to a second term.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |date=September 9, 2025 |title=Moore makes it official: Governor is running for reelection |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/wes-moore-campaign-launch-maryland-SMEOOT2ERVBPVP56EEGH3QYQMU/ |access-date=September 9, 2025 |work=The Baltimore Banner |language=en}}</ref> Moore's campaign faced token opposition in the Democratic primary and remains well funded for the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Gov. Wes Moore prepares to make case to Maryland voters for another 4 years in office |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/maryland-wes-moore-session-analysis-re-election-primary-W366Z6Q74ZG4JNMVD63GVX2OM4/ |access-date=June 18, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=April 15, 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan P. |title=Moore job approval falls below 50% for the first time |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2026/04/01/moore-job-approval-falls-below-50-for-the-first-time/ |access-date=June 18, 2026 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=April 1, 2026}}</ref> In June 2026, ahead of the primary elections, Moore began paying for advertisements to promote former state delegate and 2022 gubernatorial nominee [[Dan Cox]] in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid J. |title=Democrats Keep Helping Out This Far-Right Maryland Republican |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/16/us/politics/wes-moore-dan-cox-maryland.html |access-date=June 18, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref> Moore and Cox won their respective primaries on June 23, 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hammann |first1=Will |last2=Sears |first2=Bryan P. |title=Moore cruises to renomination as Republicans duke it out |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2026/06/23/moore-cruises-to-renomination-as-republicans-duke-it-out/ |access-date=June 24, 2026 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=June 23, 2026}}</ref> During the general election, Moore criticized Cox for supporting legislation during the 2020 legislative session to provide sales tax exemptions for [[data center]] equipment<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Moore, Cox records on MD data centers are mixed |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2026/09/10/data-centers-wes-moore-dan-cox-maryland/ |access-date=September 10, 2026 |work=The Daily Record |date=September 10, 2026}}</ref> and sought to tie Cox to President [[Donald Trump]], who endorsed Cox's gubernatorial campaign in 2022.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Governor rematch: Democrat Moore will face GOP’s Cox again |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/maryland-governor-primary-election-2026-BBC7RVV2FBAMBBNS6UZR2I27WM/ |access-date=September 10, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=June 23, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Governor of Maryland ===
{{main|Governorship of Wes Moore}}

[[File:Public Swearing In (52644811505).jpg|thumb|225px|Moore being sworn in as governor, 2023|alt=Moore takes the oath of office at his public swearing in with his wife and children]]
Moore was sworn in on January 18, 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Wes Moore to be sworn in, making history as Md.'s first Black governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/01/18/wes-moore-maryland-governor-inauguration/ |access-date=January 18, 2023 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=January 18, 2023}}</ref> He took the [[oath of office]] on a [[Bible]] owned by abolitionist [[Frederick Douglass]], as well as his grandfather's Bible.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Heim |first1=Joe |title=Maryland's governor to take oath on Frederick Douglass's Bible |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/01/14/maryland-governor-moore-frederick-douglass/ |access-date=January 14, 2023 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=January 14, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Witte |first1=Brian |title=Wes Moore to Be Sworn in as Maryland's First Black Governor |url=https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/politics/wes-moore-to-be-sworn-in-as-marylands-first-black-governor/3257285/ |access-date=January 18, 2023 |work=[[NBC Washington]] |date=January 18, 2023}}</ref> The morning before his inauguration, Moore participated in a wreath-laying ceremony at the [[Kunta Kinte]]-[[Alex Haley]] Memorial at the [[Annapolis, Maryland#Museums, historical sites, and monuments|Annapolis City Dock]] to "acknowledge the journey" that led to him becoming the third elected black governor in U.S. history.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Before becoming Maryland's first Black governor, Wes Moore will visit 'sacred place' where enslaved people once landed |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2023/01/17/before-becoming-marylands-first-black-governor-wes-moore-will-visit-sacred-place-where-enslaved-people-once-landed/ |access-date=February 1, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=January 17, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ford |first1=William J. |title=Moore joins with dignitaries at wreath laying ceremony before inauguration as state's first Black governor |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/01/18/moore-joins-with-dignitaries-at-wreath-laying-ceremony-before-inauguration-ceremony-as-states-first-black-governor/ |access-date=February 1, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=January 18, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=As Wes Moore began his first day as Maryland governor, he acknowledged the state's shameful history with slavery |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/as-wes-moore-began-his-first-day-as-maryland-governor-he-acknowledged-the-states-dark-history-with-slavery-V5ICM3Q6WBF7FKG3C2QWPHHEHY/ |access-date=February 1, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=January 18, 2023}}</ref> Later that night, he held a celebratory event at the [[Baltimore Convention Center]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kushner |first1=Kelsey |title=Wes Moore's inaugural ball attracts thousands of supporters |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/wes-moores-inaugural-ball-attracts-thousands-marylanders-supporters/ |access-date=February 1, 2023 |work=[[WJZ-TV]] |date=January 18, 2023}}</ref>

As governor, Moore testified for several of his administration's bills, making him the first governor to do so since [[Martin O'Malley]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Gov. Wes Moore testifies on veterans' tax cut bill as state lawmakers begin to consider his policy priorities |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2023/02/16/gov-wes-moore-testifies-on-veterans-tax-cut-bill-as-state-lawmakers-begin-to-consider-his-policy-priorities/ |access-date=November 8, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=February 16, 2023}}</ref> During his first term, his legislative priorities included establishing a "service year option" for high school graduates,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Moore's first bills focus on poverty, improving access to banking and broadband |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/moore-legislation-T6OVK3LHDFH7RIT76L7HA2X3TE/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=February 2, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> removing regulations around new housing development,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Miller |first1=Hallie |title=Gov. Moore housing agenda: Development, density and renter protections |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/community/housing/wes-moore-housing-plan-HOZGGH4AQVBTDKZDHXOLXZO3TA/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=January 10, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> and supporting military families through health care benefits, tax cuts, and employment opportunities.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Gov. Moore's first 2024 bills would benefit military families |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/governor-moore-military-legislation-MBVGAPML4NGBHFWIR36ZKAT4UQ/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=December 13, 2023 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |title=Wes Moore's first legislation: Tax cuts and health care for veterans |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/01/26/wes-moore-veterans-tax-break/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Washington Post |date=January 26, 2023}}</ref>

He has sought to undo or revise many of his predecessor's decisions, including the cancellation of the [[Baltimore Red Line]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Gov. Moore relaunches planning for Red Line transit in Baltimore |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/wes-moore-baltimore-red-line-W6DQUEO4VNFGJHW3YVRSPS4GT4/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=June 15, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> the withholding of state funding for training abortion care providers,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wintrode |first1=Brenda |last2=Wood |first2=Pamela |title=Gov. Wes Moore releases $69 million in withheld state funds |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/gov-wes-moore-funding-state-laws-6PZQSXULLFF43D62GBIC4DCHPQ/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=January 19, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> and plans to expand portions of the [[Capital Beltway]] and [[Interstate 270 (Maryland)|Interstate 270]] using [[high-occupancy toll lane]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lazo |first1=Luz |last2=Shepherd |first2=Katie |title=Maryland pursues publicly funding Beltway relief project |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/transportation/2023/08/21/maryland-beltway-traffic-relief-project/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=The Washington Post |date=August 21, 2023}}</ref> During the 2025 legislative session, Moore and leaders of the [[Maryland General Assembly]] negotiated and passed a spending plan that cut $2.5 billion in state spending and raised more than $1 billion in new taxes to close a $3.3 billion budget deficit.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan P. |title=Budget agreement could generate more than $1 billion in new revenue |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2025/03/20/budget-agreement-could-generate-more-than-1-billion-in-new-revenue/ |access-date=January 24, 2026 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=March 20, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan P. |title=Moore signs fiscal 2026 budget with tax increases into law in final bill signing |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2025/05/21/moore-signs-fiscal-2026-budget-with-tax-increases-into-law-in-final-bill-signing/ |access-date=January 24, 2026 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 21, 2025}}</ref>

The [[Francis Scott Key Bridge collapse]] occurred during Moore's tenure, after which he supported and signed into law legislation to provide financial assistance to workers and businesses affected by the subsequent closure of the [[Port of Baltimore]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan P. |title=Port aid, protections for highway and election workers signed into law |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2024/04/09/port-aid-protections-for-highway-and-election-workers-signed-into-law/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=Maryland Matters |date=April 9, 2024}}</ref> Following the disaster, Moore has urged [[United States Congress|Congress]] to pass legislation that would have the federal government cover the costs of [[Francis Scott Key Bridge replacement|rebuilding the bridge]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=David |title=Governor pushes Congress to pass Baltimore BRIDGE Relief Act |url=https://www.wbaltv.com/article/key-bridge-rebuild-federal-funding-baltimore-bridge-relief-act/60734935 |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=WBAL-TV |date=May 8, 2024 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Borter |first1=Gabriella |title=After bridge collapse, Maryland governor urges Congress to pass funding for rebuild |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/after-bridge-collapse-maryland-governor-urges-congress-pass-funding-rebuild-2024-03-31/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |work=Reuters |date=March 31, 2024}}</ref> In December 2024, President [[Joe Biden]] signed into law a [[continuing resolution]] bill that included a provision to fully fund the Francis Scott Key Bridge replacement.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mascaro |first1=Lisa |last2=Amiri |first2=Faroush |title=Biden signs bill that averts government shutdown, includes Key Bridge funding |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/national-politics/key-bridge-congress-shutdown-3NM7JNWNLRER7BUGADZNIKDEBY/ |access-date=December 21, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=December 20, 2024 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lebowitz |first1=Megan |title=Biden signs government funding bill, averting shutdown crisis |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/biden-signs-government-funding-bill-averting-shutdown-rcna185114 |access-date=December 21, 2024 |work=NBC News |date=December 21, 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

Moore served as the vice chair of the [[National Governors Association]] (NGA) from 2025 to 2026,<ref>{{cite news |title=Moore elected vice chair, chair-elect of National Governors Association |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2025/07/29/moore-elected-as-vice-chair-of-the-national-governors-association/ |access-date=February 8, 2026 |work=[[Daily Record (Maryland)|The Daily Record]] |date=July 29, 2025}}</ref> and as the association's chair since 2026.<ref name="NGA" /> In February 2026, President [[Donald Trump]] excluded Moore and Colorado governor [[Jared Polis]] from a bipartisan dinner event for governors and their spouses,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Gov. Moore says he's been 'singled out' by White House, disinvited from events for governors |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/national-politics/moore-trump-uninvited-white-house-PIBJQV2YEJDUTCRMLAX5UNCFJQ/ |access-date=February 8, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=February 8, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> after which the NGA said they would no longer meet with Trump and multiple other Democratic governors said they would skip the event.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Democratic governors will skip White House dinner in support of Moore |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/democratic-governors-wes-moore-white-house-dinner-KDPX3LPUIZBJTME5C4KW5DJCFM/ |access-date=February 10, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=February 10, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> In a post on [[Truth Social]], Trump confirmed he did not invite Moore while also making multiple baseless accusations against him.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Alfaro |first1=Mariana |title=Trump allows Democratic governors at White House meeting after initial snub |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/02/11/trump-democrats-governors-white-house/ |access-date=February 11, 2026 |work=[[The Washington Post]] |date=February 11, 2026}}</ref> Moore attended a NGA business meeting at the [[White House]] after the Trump administration reversed course and extended invites to Moore and Polis.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fortinsky |first1=Sarah |title=Moore, Polis to attend governors meeting after White House reverses course |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/5747241-wes-moore-trump-white-house-governors-meeting/ |access-date=February 20, 2026 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=February 20, 2026}}</ref> During his tenure as chair of the NGA, Moore launched the "Service United" initiative to encourage governors to create and expand paid public service programs in their own states.<ref name="NGA">{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=What to know about Wes Moore leading the National Governors Association |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/wes-moore-national-governors-association-chairman-IRAH7GRHCREGXMEN5OGJ7BSZ24/ |access-date=August 1, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=August 1, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

In December 2025, ''[[The Baltimore Banner]]'' reported that Moore and several members of his administration were, at times, communicating using the [[Google Chat]] platform with the "History is Off" function activated, which auto-deletes messages after 24 hours. State law requires that every unit of the state government to have policies defining which records need to be saved and which do not. In response to the ''Banner''{{'}}s findings, the Moore administration defended its use of self-deleting messages, saying that it was an "approved internal messaging feature" and "complies fully with all Maryland records laws and retention policies", but later said that the governor's office would work with the [[attorney general of Maryland]] to create a "uniform retention policy" across the executive branch.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |last2=Wintrode |first2=Brenda |title=Moore administration deleting internal messages after 24 hours |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/wes-moore-maryland-auto-deleting-records-S7BGZ2VMNFFQZGEE57HEVLXVM4/ |access-date=December 19, 2025 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=December 19, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:Public Swearing In (52644184111).jpg|thumb|225px|Moore and his family<br/>at his gubernatorial inauguration, 2023]]
Moore met [[Dawn Flythe]] in [[Washington, D.C.]] in 2002.<ref name="Limelight">{{cite news |last1=Gruskin |first1=Abigail |title=Maryland's first lady is trying to 'raise amazing human beings' in the limelight |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2024/05/08/marylands-first-lady-dawn-moore-profile/ |access-date=May 8, 2024 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=May 8, 2024}}</ref> They moved to the [[Riverside, Baltimore|Riverside]] community in [[Baltimore]] in 2006.<ref name="TaxBreaks2013" /> The couple eloped in Las Vegas while he was on a brief leave from Afghanistan and were married by an [[Elvis impersonator]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Antrim |first1=Taylor |title=Wes Moore On Gen Z, Social Media, Winning Over Republicans, and Why "Service Will Save Us" |url=https://www.vogue.com/article/wes-moore-interview-2023/amp |access-date=July 23, 2023 |work=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]] |date=July 18, 2023}}</ref> Their official wedding ceremony was held on July 6, 2007.<ref>{{cite news |title=Dawn Flythe, Westley Moore |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/08/fashion/weddings/08flythe.html |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[New York Times]] |date=July 8, 2007 |archive-date=June 3, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012440/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/08/fashion/weddings/08flythe.html |url-status=live}}</ref> They have two children, born in 2011 and 2013.<ref>{{cite news |title=Being Wes Moore |url=https://www.baltimorestyle.com/being-wes-moore/ |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=Baltimorestyle.com |date=June 17, 2015 |archive-date=October 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020123034/https://www.baltimorestyle.com/being-wes-moore/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

In late 2008, the Moores moved from Riverside to [[Guilford, Baltimore|Guilford]], where they lived until Moore's election as governor in 2022.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Maryland Gov. Wes Moore's Baltimore home sells for $2.5M |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2023/07/17/maryland-gov-wes-moores-baltimore-home-sells-for-25m/ |access-date=July 17, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 17, 2023}}</ref> From 2015 to 2023, he attended services at the Southern Baptist Church in east Baltimore.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pitts |first1=Jonathan |title=At Gov.-elect Wes Moore's last Baltimore church service before inauguration, hugs of encouragement, prayers of hope |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2023/01/16/at-gov-elect-wes-moores-last-baltimore-church-service-before-inauguration-hugs-of-encouragement-prayers-of-hope/ |access-date=January 27, 2024 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=January 16, 2023}}</ref> They reside in [[Government House (Maryland)|Government House]], the official residence of the Maryland governor and First Family in [[Annapolis, Maryland]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mosbrucker |first1=Kristen |title=Gov. Wes Moore's Baltimore City home is up for sale with $2.7M price tag |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2023-02-22/gov-wes-moores-baltimore-city-home-is-up-for-sale-with-2-7m-price-tag |access-date=February 22, 2023 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=February 22, 2023}}</ref>

Moore holds honorary degrees from [[Loyola University Maryland]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barone |first1=Katelyn |title=Veteran, Entrepreneur and Best-Selling Author Wes Moore to Deliver 2016 Commencement Speech |url=https://thegreyhound.org/5371/news/veteran-entrepreneur-and-best-selling-author-wes-moore-to-deliver-2016-commencement-speech/ |work=The Greyhound |date=February 26, 2016}}</ref> [[Lafayette College]],<ref>{{cite press release |last1=Block |first1=Dave |title=Westley Moore, Max Weinberg, and Hyman Muss '64 to Receive Honorary Degrees |url=https://news.lafayette.edu/2019/05/16/westley-moore-max-weinberg-and-hyman-muss-64-to-receive-honorary-degrees/ |access-date=November 8, 2025 |work=[[Lafayette College]] |date=May 16, 2019}}</ref> [[Skidmore College]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Campbell |first1=Ned |title=Secret's out: Oprah to speak at Skidmore commencement |url=https://www.dailygazette.com/news/secret-s-out-oprah-to-speak-at-skidmore-commencement/article_74b72626-7ce8-504e-b3d3-f030d6830748.html |access-date=January 31, 2025 |work=[[The Daily Gazette]] |date=February 23, 2017 |language=en}}</ref> [[Lincoln University (Pennsylvania)|Lincoln University]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=Chanel |title=Lincoln names Maryland Gov. Moore as keynote speaker for graduation |url=https://www.phillytrib.com/news/state_and_region/lincoln-names-maryland-gov-moore-as-keynote-speaker-for-graduation/article_f24584ff-5e40-594f-a0fb-6796e0f1de86.html |access-date=March 11, 2025 |work=[[The Philadelphia Tribune]] |date=March 10, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> and the [[University of the Commonwealth Caribbean]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davidson |first1=Vernon |title=Jamaica gives me a deep sense of clarity, says Maryland Governor Wes Moore |url=https://www.jamaicaobserver.com/news/jamaica-gives-me-a-deep-sense-of-clarity-says-maryland-governor-wes-moore/amp/ |access-date=July 23, 2023 |work=[[Jamaica Observer]] |date=July 18, 2023}}</ref> He is a member of the [[Sons of the American Revolution]]; his ancestor Prince Ames served in the [[Massachusetts Militia]] in the Revolutionary War.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pittman |first1=Elijah |last2=Ford |first2=William J. |last3=Sears |first3=Bryan P. |title=MACo Matters: Ferguson renews stance against broad-based tax increases |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2024/08/15/maco-matters-ferguson-renews-stance-against-broad-based-tax-increases/ |access-date=August 16, 2024 |work=Maryland Matters |date=August 16, 2024}}</ref>

Moore is a fan of the [[Baltimore Ravens]], [[Baltimore Orioles]], and [[New York Knicks]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid J. |last2=Glueck |first2=Katie |last3=Browning |first3=Kellen |title=Democrats Turn to Sports Radio and Podcasts to Try to Reach Young Men |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/09/us/politics/democrats-sports-podcasts-male-voters.html |access-date=March 9, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 9, 2025}}</ref>

In June 2013, an investigation by ''[[The Baltimore Sun]]'' alleged that Moore was improperly receiving [[Homestead exemption|homestead property tax credits]] and owed back taxes to the city of Baltimore. Moore told ''The Sun'' that he was unaware of any issues with the home's taxes and wanted to pay what he owed immediately.<ref name="TaxBreaks2013">{{cite news |last1=Calvert |first1=Scott |title=Author Wes Moore got undeserved tax breaks |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2013/06/18/author-wes-moore-got-undeserved-tax-breaks/ |access-date=June 2, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=June 18, 2013 |archive-date=June 3, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603012454/https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/investigations/bal-author-wes-moore-got-undeserved-homestead-credits-20130614-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In October 2022, ''[[Baltimore Brew]]'' reported that Moore had not paid any water and sewage charges since March 2021, owing $21,200 to the city of Baltimore.<ref name="Water bills">{{cite news |last1=Reutter |first1=Mark |title=EXCLUSIVE: Maryland gubernatorial candidate Wes Moore owes $21,000 in delinquent Baltimore City water bills |url=https://www.baltimorebrew.com/2022/10/05/exclusive-maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-wes-moore-owes-21000-in-delinquent-baltimore-city-water-bills/ |access-date=October 5, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Brew]] |date=October 5, 2022 |archive-date=October 6, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221006001949/https://www.baltimorebrew.com/2022/10/05/exclusive-maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-wes-moore-owes-21000-in-delinquent-baltimore-city-water-bills/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Moore settled his outstanding bills shortly after the article was published.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jensen |first1=Cassidy |title=Wes Moore settled $21K in unpaid Baltimore water bills Wednesday, spokesman says |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/10/06/maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-wes-moore-settles-21k-baltimore-water-bill-balance-he-was-unaware-of-spokesman-says/ |access-date=October 6, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=October 5, 2022 |archive-date=October 6, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221006040654/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-wes-moore-water-bills-20221006-rxdtufx6gbenjdukrueb7fvz3y-story.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

